course: dictionary
lesson: 8507
title: Words with attitude
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words show how a character feels, not just what happens. Look at the words around them. «Don't give me any sass!» is what a parent might say to a child who answers back, so sass must mean cheeky talk.
explain: The same word can be a telling-off or a compliment, so check who is speaking and how. «She's got sass» is praise: it means she is bold, lively and confident. Ask yourself: is someone being told off, or admired?
explain: Sass is informal and mainly used in the USA. In the UK people more often say «cheek» or «lip». If you meet sass in a book, swap in cheek and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Sass can be a noun or a verb: you can have sass, or you can sass someone.
fact: «Sass» means cheeky, rude talk back, especially to a parent or teacher.
fact: «Sass» can also praise someone's bold, lively confidence and style.
fact: Sass is informal and mainly used in the USA; in the UK people often say «cheek» or «lip».
fact: Check the clues: a telling-off means cheek, while a compliment means confidence.
exercise: «Don't give me any sass!» What does sass mean here? | A sweet cake | Silent staring | Bright, shiny paint | *Cheeky talk back
exercise: «She's got sass.» Is this a telling-off or a compliment? | A telling-off, it means rude talk | A telling-off, it means lost | *A compliment, it means bold, lively confidence | Neither, it means sleepy
exercise: Which everyday word do many people in the UK use for cheeky talk back? | *Cheek | Garden | Whistle | Pancake
test: Sass is mainly used in which place? | Ancient Rome | The Moon | *The USA | Antarctica
test: In «Don't sass me», what kind of word is sass? | *A verb | A number | A place name | An adjective
exam: In «She's full of sass», what does sass most likely mean? | A type of sandwich | Feeling sleepy | Cheeky, rude talk | *Bold, lively confidence and style
